 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery via self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed changes are pushed to connected browsers over persistent WebSockets rather than short-polling or a hosted realtime vendor. Avoids vendor lock-in and ongoing third-party service costs while delivering low-latency updates, in exchange for managing connection lifecycle, server socket capacity, and client reconnection/catch-up logic directly. Alternative short-polling was rejected due to repeated read load and latency; hosted realtime was rejected due to recurring fees and external provider coupling. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
